    {
      "title": "Maharashtra declares drought in 265 talukas, announces relief measures",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/maharashtra-declares-drought-in-265-talukas-announces-relief-measures-126092600678_1.html",
      "description": "The Maharashtra government on Saturday declared drought in 265 of its 358 talukas, or 74 per cent of the state, amid rainfall deficit and announced relief measures, including crop loan restructuring, land revenue concessions, and power bill subsidies.\nThe government will announce financial assistance for crop losses after the prescribed procedure.\nAccording to a government resolution (GR) issued late on Friday, 265 talukas spread across 32 districts cover 74 per cent of the state. At 74, Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar division has the highest number of drought-affected talukas.\nAnother government order issued on Saturday said that a separate GR will be issued on financial assistance for crop losses suffered by farmers in the 265 talukas after the prescribed procedure.\nChief Minister Devendra Fadnavis has directed district collectors to initiate crop-damage assessments immediately and assured that farmers remain the centre of the government's relief efforts.\nDeputy CM Sunetra Pawar said",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-26T12:55:47"
    },
    {
      "title": "Maharashtra declares 265 of 358 talukas drought-hit, announces relief",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/maharashtra-declares-265-of-358-talukas-drought-hit-announces-relief-126092600381_1.html",
      "description": "The Maharashtra government on Saturday declared 265 of the 358 talukas in the state as drought-affected, announcing relief measures, including restructuring of crop loans, concession in land revenue and deferment of recovery of agriculture-related loans.\nAccording to a government resolution issued late on Friday, this covers 74 per cent of the state.\nThe decision follows Chief Minister Devendra Fadnavis's recent visit to some drought-affected parts of the state.\nThe state government has activated 'Trigger-1' under its manual for drought management 2016 (updated in 2020) and Drought Management Code to declare drought conditions and initiate relief.\nTrigger-1 is the first stage in the state's drought framework, activated by a rainfall deficit of more than 25 per cent combined with a prolonged dry spell of 21 days.\nA report submitted by the Agriculture Commissioner found drought-like conditions in the 265 talukas across 32 districts due to rainfall deficit and other factors since ...",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-26T09:22:44"
    }
